A||N||11||Monkeyman||Another new mod||||7||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||12-02-1999||12:09 AM||||This is for those of you who want to upgrade to power mirrors.  Use the joystick control out of a late'80s/very early '90s Ford Escort (sorry-I don't know what they were called in Canada or elswhere).  The control is mounted in the middle of the door.  I just found one today for $5 at the boneyard.  It's in the same type of sleeve as the manual mirror joystick in a Fiero.  It will fit in the Fiero sleeve (tight fit).  This will save you having to find something to cover the hole if you were to use an OEM control (in the console).  This may not seem like much, but it kinda looks cool.||||reg||7
Z||000001||mwbackus||12-02-1999||07:02 AM||||With the inner door panels specific to the power mirror option becoming more and more rare to find in pristine condition, I think could be a VERY important application.  Since I am unfamiliar with the idea of employing the Escort PM controls on the Fiero, I have some questions.  How does this control operate the passenger side mirror?  How does the wiring interface with the PM harness of the Fiero, seeing that the actual PM control for the Fiero is in the console?  Does it replace this harness entirely?<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in user profile)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||12-02-1999||10:41 AM||||Unfortunately, you've gotten way ahead of me.  Or maybe I've gotten way ahead of myself.  1st-the joystick twists left to control the left mirror and right to control the right mirror.  I don't know how the wiring would interface with the stock wiring as I don't even have power mirrors yet.  Sorry.  This is an upgrade I plan on doing as soon as I can find the appropriate mirror heads.  I knew I would have to make my own wiring harness, so I hadn't given that much thought.  Sorry, again.  And, no.  It wouldn't replace the harness at all.  The piece I got was just the control with about 6" of wires ending in a plug.  I should've taken more time, removed the door panel to the donor car and cut a good length of wire, too, but the boneyard was about to close.  I did call my local Ford dealer and ask about a wiring harness (just to get the other side of the plug and some wire) and they said that the only harness I could get was the entire piece up to the firewall for $265.  I plan to cut the wires at the plug and attach an aftermarket plug.  This has 8 wires and I've seen aftermarket plugs with that capacity.  Once I get this wired up (need to get the mirrors 1st) I'll post the wiring diagram.  Ford didn't know.  They said that I would need the specific year of the vehicle as the wiring sometimes changed between years.  As soon as I get my film developed, I'll try to post some pics of this piece in case anybody else wants to try this.  BTW-incase you can't find this at your boneyard, Ford still carries the part for $43.  Another BTW-once this is installed, you can't tell it's not stock unless you really look.  What I mean is,  although it's not exactly the same as the manual joystick, it doesn't stand out.  As I've said before in some other threads...It's all in the details.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||12-02-1999||11:58 AM||||The wiring between the new ford control and the fiero mirrors should be npo problem. The switch controls the ground. It either groungs the driver side or the passenger side. Both side get a hot signal when a direction is selected. The fiero switch is the same design. Good work monkey. I went for a year with  a hole till I found a pm door panel after I did the upgrade.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||12-02-1999||01:07 PM||||This same switch is in a lot of Fords - my dad's 83 5-liter Thunderbird has one.<P>(Dad thinks his Bird is fast, but I don't have the heart to tell him his TBI V8 barely puts out more HP than my 86 SE.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||mwbackus||12-02-1999||01:42 PM||||Well, this is exactly the stuff I like to see.  Ingenuity is important when they no longer make various new replacement parts.  Post all the picks you can get, please.<P>Mike<BR>(Fiero info in user profile)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||12-02-1999||05:13 PM||||How does the switch control the ground?  I noticed that the control has a red and a black wire and 6 others of various colors.  I assume the red wire is for the power and the black one is for the ground.  I figured on running the ground to a screw on the door and getting the power from the fuse box.  The other wires should be easy to check with a test lite, wouldn't they?  I don't see how 6 wires can control 8 different actions, though (4 on each side).  Are there some common wires.  (?One wire controls "up" on both sides or something like that?)  I don't know  much about this so I would appreciate any suggestions.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||12-03-1999||09:28 AM||||Controlling the ground means that the switch completes the circuit to ground for whatever direction you want the mirror to move.  +12 could be supplied to each door, but current does not flow through the selected circuit until you move the switch which will complete a path to ground.  It shouldnt be too hard to determine which wire does what with the help of a meter.  Just set it to test continuity and connect one lead to the ground and another lead to any other wire and move the stick around.  <P>As for the 6 wires, they could be configured as follows: (and I make no mention of wire colors, you need a schematic for that.  Or a Ford owner, my mom would get upset if I dismantled her Lincoln!)<P>Ground (or +12V)<BR>Mirror Select<BR>Mirror Up<BR>Mirror Down<BR>Mirror Left<BR>Mirror Right<P>Hope this helps!  As for the power mirror assemblies, most any GM models with sport style power mirrors should do.  Especially since your going to have to rewire it anyway.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||12-03-1999||04:35 PM||||You probably can determine which wire does what by using an ohm meter.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||12-03-1999||08:19 PM||||Actually, "ringing out" the switch could be a pain. there are a number of ways to connect the swiches in the control. 6 wires makes it interesting.<P>It's been awhile since i had a power mirroor apart. I'm doing this from what I remember. I don't recall GM using any relays or other controls in the system.<P>First, the mirrors use 2 motors each. The power is reversed to change direction.<P>So that being the case. In the mirror,<P>2 wires go to the up/down motor.<BR>2 wires go to the left/right motor.<BR>But one wire in the harness to the mirror is shared. Only 3 wires go to the mirrors.<P>If this switch was wired the "simple" way, which is unlikely, then:<P>1 wire is +<BR>1 wire is ground<BR>2 wires are up/down<BR>2 wires are left right.<P>This of course ignores the select switch. To include the select in 6 wires. And this is only one of a couple posibilities,,,,,<P>One wire should be +<P>one wire is likely ground.<P>one  wire goes to each mirror. these are the select lines. This wire is connected to both motors in the mirror. (2 wires used here.)<P>One wire goes to + from BOTH up/down motors. <P>one wire goes to + from BOTH left/right motors.<P>To work a motor the select has to be set for that mirror and the contects in the direction switch must close. for each motor to move a total of 3 contacts must close.<P>If the direction switch allows diagonals then it is simply running both motors in the mirror together.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||12-05-1999||10:27 PM||||OK...1st, I just bought a pair of complete doors over the weekend.  They have power mirrors, windows, and locks.  I had to gut the doors to get them home (long story-ask me later if you're really interested).  I tested the mirrors before I took them (still attached to the car) and the up, down, and left of both mirrors works fine.  The right on both didn't work at all, so I'm assuming that it's in the switch.  If there are 4 wires to each mirror head and a total of 8 wires from my switch (red, black and 6 others)...well, I'm just confused.  I've never done anything like this before.  <P>Next are the "new" p/windows.  I couldn't check these as the ignition key was lost.  I assume the key either needs to be "ON" or in the "ACC" position to work.  I will install the switches in the center console, so can I assume that the wiring diagram in my Haynes manual will tell all?  If not, please post anything I need to know.<P>Last are the p/locks.  These work, although they seemed to be a little slow and they acted like they didn't want to lock/unlock the doors all the way every time.  (I worked them 4 or 5 times)  Can I assume that a little cleaning and some lithium grease on the actuators will help?  And, since the switches and the actuators are all together (at least in the doors themselves), do I just run a wire between the 2 doors?  Or do I need to get complicated?  <P>Last (at least for now)...Are all Fieros wired for all possible options?  I have the wiring harnesses.  Can I just plug n play?  Sorry I've "talked" so long, but I need to get this stuff figured out.  I'd like to get this done this week while I'm still off of work.  Thanx for everything, guys.  ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-06-1999||12:26 AM||||OK--It's 2hrs later, I've taken my wife to work and I can start working on these problems.  I have both the p/mirror and the main (from the door) wiring harnesses in my hand.  The wiring harness from the mirror has 4 wires and the other one has 7 wires to the body of the car broken down as follows:  3 to the p/lock switch on the door (lt. blue, orange, and black), 2 to the p/lock actuator (gray and brown w/ gray stripe) and 2 to the window regulator (dk. blue and brown-these are bigger than the rest).  Now...can anyone figure out what I'm supposed to do with these?  (please see last message)  Thanx, again.||||reg||1
